<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raghunatha Nayak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1600">1600</a>–<a href="/years/1634">1634</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raghunatha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> was the most celebrated ruler of the Nayak kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> in the Kaveri delta. A loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, he fought for it in its succession war, notably at Toppur in 1616. A poet and musician, he made his court a centre of Telugu literature and Carnatic music.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raghunatha, a Telugu king and the third ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> from the Nayak dynasty, was the eldest son of Achuthappa Nayak. The Raghunathabhyudayam and the Sahityanatyakara describe his boyhood training in the shastras, warfare and administration. His chief queen was Kalavati, and Ramabhadramba, who wrote a history of the dynasty, was one of his concubines. He won early acclaim fighting the Golconda Sultanate, and from 1600 he ruled jointly with his father, becoming sole ruler in 1614.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1614 the Vijayanagar emperor Sriranga II was killed by a rival claimant, Jaggaraya, and Raghunatha set out to avenge him. He first put down Solaga, a feudatory of Krishnappa Nayak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a> described as a highwayman, whose island stronghold near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbakonam">Kumbakonam</a> fell to Raghunatha's artillery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> forces then drove the Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> and placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cankili-ii">Cankili II</a> of the Aryachakravarti dynasty on its throne; Portuguese records say the expedition was led by a general named Khem Nayak. At Toppur Raghunatha defeated Jaggaraya, who was captured and killed, and Rama Deva Raya was put on the throne of Vijayanagar.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cankili-ii">Cankili II</a> was overthrown and killed in 1619, and five armies Raghunatha sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> between 1619 and 1621 failed. He kept cordial relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and the English; the Danish settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ove-gjedde">Ove Gjedde</a> on 19 November 1620. An English mission landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaikal">Karaikal</a> on 23 May 1624 and received leave to trade, but Raghunatha later withdrew it and demanded an annual rent of 7,000 riyals. His inscriptions are found as far afield as Ramanathapuram, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a> districts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Raghunatha played the veena, whose structure changed in his time, and is credited with the raga Jayantasena and the tala Ramananda. He composed Yakshaganas and Telugu kavyas, among them Parijatapaharanamu, Valmikicharitram and a Ramayanam, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works Sangita Sudha and Bharatha Sudha. The poets Ramabhadramba, Madhuravani, Chemakura Venkataraju and Krishnadhwari worked at his court, and the composer Kshetrayya wrote padas there. He built the Ramaswamy Temple and the gopura of the Adi Kumbeswarar Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbakonam">Kumbakonam</a> and the Saraswathi Mahal Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, and expanded the Uppiliappan and Rajagopalaswamy temples.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raghunatha Nayak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Danish expedition under Ove Gjedde reached south India, and in 1620 <b>Raghunatha Nayak</b> of Thanjavur granted the Danish East India Company the village of Tranquebar (Tharangambadi).</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-india">Danish India</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Raghunatha Nayak</b>, the most celebrated ruler, was a poet, musician and patron who supported Vijayanagara in its last wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/thanjavur-nayak-kingdom">Thanjavur Nayak kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.79,79.14&amp;year=1617" aria-label="Thanjavur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6730 1761 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Raghunatha Nayak"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7180 2031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7180 2031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.79,79.14&amp;year=1617">What was here in 1617? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
